{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>When your brother entices you, etc.</b> In this parashah HaShem hinted at a great allusion regarding the modes of enticement that come to a person from himself, to turn him from the good and upright path. Know that there is an enticer that comes with a person from the day he comes into the world, as they said of blessed memory (Sanhedrin 91a) on the verse (Genesis 4:7) ״לַפֶּתַח חַטָּאת רֹבֵץ״ — and it entices a person to do evil." "<b>Further,</b> a second enticing force is born within a person by means of the transgression itself. If he transgressed one commandment, there is born in him a force that desires wickedness — the secret of the saying (Avot 4:2) עֲבֵרָה גּוֹרֶרֶת עֲבֵרָה (one transgression drags in another). And the men of truth elaborated on this, saying that through one transgression a person acquires one prosecutor, and it too is added to the first evil force to cause him to sin. And likewise two, and likewise three — when he sins much, the enticement grows great within him. And for this reason they said (Berakhot 34a): the place where penitents stand, even the perfectly righteous cannot stand." "<b>Further,</b> by means of a person's inclining toward a path that is not good, a third force will surround him, which is an evil soul. Just as a person merits an additional soul when he grows strong in the ways of HaShem, so too, by means of evil paths, he acquires an additional evil soul to cause him to sin." "<b>Further,</b> from this a fourth fence will be added, and it is that his soul enters into another category beyond which there is nothing higher — namely, that his soul joins and unites with the portion of evil and becomes equal to it in its nature. This is the complete and final devastation of a person's soul, to deny the fundamental principle and to serve idolatry." "<b>And this is</b> what He hinted at by saying ״כִּי יְסִיתְךָ <b>אָחִיךָ</b>״ — it is the portion of evil that becomes like a brother to a person from the very beginning, before he comes into the world. And His saying <b>בֶּן אִמֶּךָ</b> intends to inform us from where this brother in trouble came: He said <b>בֶּן אִמֶּךָ</b> — she is the mother of all living, namely Chavah, who caused the admixture of evil into the source of holiness through the first sin. And corresponding to the second fence, which is the enticer born from a person's sin as mentioned above, He said <b>אוֹ בִנְךָ אוֹ בִתְּךָ</b> — they are the offspring of evil deeds, which are also called strange children. And He said <b>אוֹ בִתְּךָ</b> because there are transgressions that give birth to a prosecutor in the category of male and there are in the category of female, according to the power of the sin: when it is great it gives birth to the force of the male, and according to its weakness the designation of daughter is applied to it." "<b>And corresponding</b> to the third safeguard, which is the acquisition of an evil soul that attaches itself to the sinner, He said <b>or the wife of your bosom</b> — for an evil soul dwells within him and intensifies in him the incitement to activate evil." "<b>And corresponding</b> to the fourth safeguard, which is the inclination and longing of the soul toward the evil portion that may be within his soul, He said <b>your friend who is like your own soul</b> — for there is no distinction between the two of them except that the evil portion and his soul are a single entity. And after these gradations the inciter will have power to speak to him words such as these: ״נֵלְכָה וְנַעַבְדָה״ etc." "And He was precise to say <b>in secret</b>, to alert that the inciter conceals himself because he is an inciter, and speaks as one speaking words of love and friendship, and shows talons as though he does not intend harm." "And the verse's saying <b>to say, Let us go</b>, means not that he says explicitly ״נֵלְכָה נַעַבְדָה,״ but rather he speaks concealed things, and the culmination of his words is to say <b>נֵלְכָה נַעַבְדָה</b> etc. He also cleverly frames his statement to say ״נֵלְכָה נַעַבְדָה,״ which includes himself with him, and thereby deviously implies that he too until now had not consented to idolatry but from this point onward wishes to worship — and through this the person will think that his own soul is what is speaking, and nothing other than it, meaning the evil inclination." "And the verse's saying <b>which you have not known.</b> That is, he comes with a claim against what the person obligated himself to upon receiving the Torah — not to serve other elohim — and says to him that the obligation applies only to idolatry that he knew about and regarding which he accepted not to worship it; but idolatry of elohim that were not known in the world — there is no claim against him based on what he accepted not to worship, for this never entered his mind at the time he accepted. And this is what the verse says: <b>which you have not known</b> — and not you alone, but <b>and your fathers</b> also, when they accepted upon themselves the prohibition of idolatry, did not know this one."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {7 "טְלָפַיִם literally means 'claws/talons'; the image is of a predator hiding its claws to appear harmless. Translated literally."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:17:24.917899745Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}